::: notes
This is the grid from the first session, with the SCORE study that sits in each cell. Same data and the same methods, top left, is the reproducibility study: six hundred papers, of which a hundred and forty-three could be assessed in the end. Same data and different methods, bottom left, is Multi100, the robustness study, with a hundred studies and five hundred and four reanalyses. New data and the same methods, top right, is the replicability study, with two hundred and seventy-four claims tested on data that the original studies did not use. The fourth cell, generalisation, has no SCORE study, and we leave it aside as before. We take the three in turn, starting with reproduction.
:::

## Reproducibility – Miske et al.

::: {.small}
- **600 papers** sampled (62 journals, 2009–2018)
- The authors asked for their data and code, and the reported result rerun from them
- The first question: how often is that even possible?
:::

::: {.src}
@MiskeEtAl2026InvestigatingReproducibilitySocial.
:::

::: notes
Reproducibility is the gentlest test of the three. The design in one breath: take six hundred papers, ask the authors for their data and code, and try to recompute the central reported result from the same data with the same analysis. Where the authors did not supply data, the team tried to rebuild the dataset from the source data, which is a much harder job. Before we ask how often a reproduction works, the first finding is about how rarely you can even start.
:::

## Reproducibility results – Miske et al. {#most-papers-never-reach-the-starting-line}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](score-multi100_files/figure-revealjs/miske-waterfall-1.png){width=816}
:::
:::


::: {.small}
- Of the **143 assessed**: **53.6%** reproduced precisely, **73.5%** at least approximately
- Counting the papers with no data available, **17.6%** were at least approximately reproducible
:::

::: {.src}
@MiskeEtAl2026InvestigatingReproducibilitySocial, pp. 126 and 131. The 143 assessed papers come from 182 datasets, 38 of them rebuilt from source data.
:::

::: notes
Read it left to right. Six hundred papers sampled. Data were available for twenty-four per cent of them, and for nearly three quarters there was neither data nor code. The team rebuilt thirty-eight more datasets from source data, which made a hundred and eighty-two, and assessed 143 of them. Of those, just over half reproduce precisely, and about three quarters at least approximately. That sounds respectable until you count the papers that nobody could check as failures, and then the rate falls to seventeen point six per cent, a figure that the paper itself reports beside the higher one. Most of the loss happens at the first step, getting hold of the data.
:::

## Data availability – Miske et al. {#why-some-fields-do-better}

::: {.small}
- Journals with a sharing policy: **66.2–100%** data availability vs **16.0%** without
- By discipline: **54.1%** data availability in political science vs **2.9%** in education
- With data *and* code: **90.9%** approximately reproducible vs **38.1%** from rebuilt source data
:::

::: {.claimcard}
"The most substantial barrier … was the unavailability of author data."

"Reproducibility success does not mean that the finding is correct."
:::

::: {.src}
@MiskeEtAl2026InvestigatingReproducibilitySocial, pp. 127, 129, 130 and 131.
:::

::: notes
And access follows policy. Where journals have a data-sharing policy, availability runs from about two thirds to one hundred per cent, depending on the policy; without one, it is sixteen. By field, political science and economics are far ahead of the rest, and education comes last. The first quotation opens the discussion of the paper: the biggest barrier was simply that the data were not there. The second is the one to remember: "reproducibility success does not mean that the finding is correct." Reproducing a number tells you that the computation was faithful, and nothing about whether the claim about the world is true.
:::

## Robustness – Aczel et al. (Multi100)

::: {.small}
- **100 studies**, each reanalysed by **at least 5 independent analysts**: 457 analysts and 504 reanalyses in all
- The same data for every analyst of a study, each free to choose any *justifiable* analysis
- A panel of peers judged whether each analysis was appropriate
- I was one of its analysts, on the paper of the EU-frames case
:::

::: {.src}
@AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness, p. 136.
:::

::: notes
The middle paper is the one that I took part in, as one of the five analysts of the paper of the EU-frames case, one of the four cases of this course. One hundred studies, and for each one at least five analysts working independently, everyone handed the same data and the same claim. You were free to analyse it in any way you could justify, and a panel of peers later judged whether each analysis was appropriate. Most of the hundred studies had been through a reproduction check by SCORE before Multi100 began, and for nine of them the reproduction was never attempted. The record of the four cases of the course, which closes this session, shows how differently those checks turned out, case by case.
:::

## Robustness results – Aczel et al. {#same-data-five-analysts-different-answers}

::: {.small}
- **34%** of the reanalysis effect sizes within ±0.05 *d* of the original (57% within ±0.20 *d*)
- **74%** reached the same conclusion as the original study, 24% a null or inconclusive one, **2%** the opposite
- Effect sizes shrank: a mean *d* of **0.73** in the original studies, **0.49** in the reanalyses
- Observational studies came out less robust than experiments: **28%** against **44%** of the reanalyses within ±0.05 *d*
:::

::: {.src}
@AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness, pp. 136–139.
:::

::: notes
Free, justifiable choices on the same data move the answer a long way. The d here is a standardised effect size, Cohen's d, and how every analysis was converted to it is the subject of the next section. Only about a third of the reanalyses fell within five hundredths of a d of the original, and in just five of the ninety-five studies whose original effect size could be recovered did every analyst come that close. Conclusions held up better than magnitudes, with three quarters of the reanalyses agreeing with the original conclusion. But the effect sizes shrank, from a mean d of point seven three to point four nine. And observational studies came out less robust than experiments, which matters here, because three of the four cases of this course are observational studies, all but the housing audit.
:::

## Replicability – Tyner et al.

::: {.small}
- **274 claims** from 164 papers, tested on **independent data**: newly collected for 128 claims, existing data for 146
- A median statistical power of **99.6%**, a design peer reviewed in advance, and a plan preregistered on the OSF
- The strictest test: does the effect reappear in data that the original study did not use?
:::

::: {.src}
@TynerEtAl2026InvestigatingReplicabilitySocial, pp. 143 and 148, and Methods.
:::

::: notes
The third paper is the strictest test, because each claim is checked against data that the original study did not use, newly collected for 128 claims and existing data for the other 146. Two hundred and seventy-four claims, each replication designed by a team, peer reviewed in advance, with an author of the original study among the reviewers whenever one agreed to take part, and preregistered before it ran. The median statistical power was ninety-nine point six per cent. At that power an effect as large as the original one would be very hard to miss, so a claim that fails here cannot be put down to a study too small to detect it.
:::

## Replicability results – Tyner et al. {#even-success-shrinks}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](score-multi100_files/figure-revealjs/tyner-shrink-1.png){width=720}
:::
:::


::: {.small}
- **55.1%** of claims replicated by the significance criterion, and effect sizes more than **halved**
- Thirteen ways of judging success give from **28.6%** to **74.8%**
:::

::: {.src}
@TynerEtAl2026InvestigatingReplicabilitySocial, pp. 143, 146 and 148.
:::

::: notes
And this is what came back. The median effect size more than halves: point two five in the originals, point one zero in the replications. By the significance criterion, fifty-five per cent of claims replicate. Whether a claim replicated is not a single question, either: the team scored the same studies against thirteen reasonable criteria and got answers from twenty-nine to seventy-five per cent. As the paper puts it, "Strictly speaking, there is no such thing as exact replication." And later: "The problem to solve is not unreplicability per se, it is overconfidence."
:::

## The three Rs across SCORE {#the-three-rs-do-not-travel-together}

::: {.claimcard}
"The correlations between repeatability outcomes were not distinguishable from zero in this sample given the methods of the program."
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Knowing that a finding was reproduced tells you next to nothing about whether it is robust or whether it replicates. For any one paper, the question is which of the three it was put through, and how it fared.
:::

::: {.src}
@NosekEtAl2025BriefGlossaryTerms [p. 2]
:::

::: notes
You would hope that these three properties go together, that a reproducible finding is more likely to be robust and a robust one more likely to replicate. Across SCORE they do not, or at least not detectably. In the words of the glossary that we met in the first session, the correlations between the three kinds of outcome were not distinguishable from zero. So there is no single trust score for a finding. "Is it reliable?" is an incomplete question until you say which kind of repetition you mean, and for any one paper the useful question is which of the three tests it was put through and how it fared. We put exactly that question to the four cases of the course at the end of the session.
:::

# Multi100 {#multi100 .darkslide background-color="rgba(0,0,0,0)"}

::: {.partlabel}
Section 2
:::

::: notes
Section two goes inside Multi100, the robustness study, because its design is the design that this course repeats, with you in the place of its analysts. We take it in the order in which an analyst met it: how the claims were chosen, what arrived with each claim, and the two tasks, the free one and the constrained one. Then the step that makes a hundred studies comparable at all, the conversion of every result to a common scale, and an assumption of that conversion which is easy to miss, shown on the case that I know best.
:::

## How Multi100 chose its claims {#how-multi100-chose-its-claims}

::: {.small}
- **One claim per paper**, from the first phase of SCORE: a sentence of the abstract that "ideally corresponded to one statistically significant inferential test result"
- **Four checks.** The lead team kept claims that were understandable, held only one claim, indicated the direction of the effect and had a corresponding test in the article
- **Ellipses.** An expression judged "ambiguous or rhetorical" was replaced by an ellipsis, "while preserving the original wording and the meaning of the claim"
- **No research question.** The analysts received the claim, and no research question to answer
:::

::: {.src}
Multi100 preregistration, osf.io/4ev9t, p. 5.
:::

::: notes
Multi100 did not choose its claims from scratch. It took them from the first phase of SCORE, in which a single claim had been traced in the abstract of each paper, ideally one that matched a single significant test. The lead team then checked each claim: was it understandable, was it one claim and not several, did it state a direction, and was there a test in the article that corresponded to it? Where a phrase was judged ambiguous or rhetorical, it was replaced by an ellipsis, so that "dramatically increased" became "increased" with three dots before it, and three of the four claims of this course carry such dots. And the analysts got no research question, because the lead team judged that turning a claim into a question would push the analysts towards its own reading of the claim.
:::

## What a Multi100 analyst received {#inside-multi100-what-an-analyst-was-sent}

::: {.small}
- A **published paper**, in full and not blinded, so the original estimates were in view
- Its **central claim**, in one sentence
- The **data**, or directions for fetching them
- No instructions on method: any analysis that you can defend
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
And two things that no analyst was told: **who the other analysts were**, and **what they were finding**.
:::

::: {.src}
@AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness; Multi100 preregistration, osf.io/4ev9t, pp. 4–5.
:::

::: notes
Multi100 handed a hundred published studies to at least five independent analysts each, and I was one of those analysts, on the paper of the EU-frames case. Let me describe what that was like from the inside, because it shapes how you read the results. What arrived was the published paper, the whole thing and not blinded, so the original estimates were in view, and if anything that pulls you towards the original answer. With it came the central claim of the paper in a single sentence, and the data or directions for fetching them. In my case that meant sixteen Eurobarometer waves that I had to fetch from GESIS myself, under licence. What did not arrive was any instruction on method. And two things stayed hidden throughout: who the other analysts were, and what any of them were finding.
:::

## The two Multi100 tasks {#two-tasks-months-apart .smaller}

::: {.small}
**Task 1 – free reanalysis.** Reanalyse the claim however you see fit, then record one conclusion: evidence for the claim, evidence for the opposite, no evidence either way, or evidence for the null hypothesis.
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
**Task 2 – constrained reanalysis.** Once Task 1 was in, report a single main result from the family of *z*, *t*, *F* or $\chi^2$ tests, following instructions for the claim on matters such as data selection and exclusions.
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Task 1 measures whether analysts reach the same verdict. Task 2 makes their *numbers* comparable, since five answers to five different questions cannot be averaged.
:::

::: {.src}
Multi100 preregistration, osf.io/4ev9t, pp. 7–8; @AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness, Methods.
:::

::: notes
The study ran in two stages, and the distinction matters for everything that follows. Task one was complete freedom: reanalyse the claim however you see fit, and record one categorical conclusion out of four, evidence for the claim, evidence for the opposite, no evidence either way, or evidence for the null hypothesis. [advance] Once that was submitted, and it could no longer be changed, task two arrived. It asked for a single main result in the form of a test statistic, and it came with instructions for the claim, on matters such as which data to use and what to exclude. [advance] The two tasks measure different things. Task one asks whether independent analysts reach the same verdict. Task two asks whether they produce the same number, and that question can only be asked once everyone is answering roughly the same question, which is what the instructions were for.
:::

## The instructions in the four cases {#the-instructions-in-the-four-cases .smaller .deepdive run="instructions"}

::: {.fs-75}
| Case | What the instruction of Task 2 asked |
|:----|:--------------|
| Housing audit | to leave the ethnic composition of the communities out of the model |
| Immigration and trust | to include unhappiness among the controls, to measure trust in politics by trust in parliament, and to leave out two interactions with concern about immigration |
| Cohabitation | to leave out the interactions between education and period |
| EU frames | to leave out the contextual variables on EU policies and on the positions of parties, to use the positive non-materialist form of EU framing, and to measure poor economic performance by the unemployment rate |
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
The preregistration required the instructions to "remain circumstantial (e.g., data selection, exclusions) and never give direct instructions to the choice of statistical approach or full specification of the model". The published Methods add "choice of measurement" to the examples.
:::

::: {.src}
The Multi100 record of each claim (osf.io/q5h2c); preregistration, osf.io/4ev9t, p. 6; @AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness, Methods.
:::

::: notes
Here are the four instructions side by side, in my words rather than theirs; on the second day you read the exact wording for your own case. They differ a good deal. The cohabitation instruction removes one set of interactions and says nothing else, and the housing-audit instruction removes the ethnic composition of the communities. The instructions for the immigration-and-trust and EU-frames claims go further and choose measures: the outcome in both cases, and for the EU-frames claim the predictor as well. [advance] The preregistration allowed only instructions that it called circumstantial, about data selection and exclusions, and never about the statistical approach or the full model. The published Methods add the choice of measurement to the examples, and the EU-frames instruction, which I received myself, makes two choices of exactly that kind.
:::

## Results on a common scale {#how-a-hundred-studies-become-one-number}

::: {.small}
- Every analyst reports a test statistic in whatever form the model produces – **t**, **z**, **F**, $\chi^2$ – with its degrees of freedom or its sample size
- Each is converted to a **partial correlation** *r* and then to a standardised **Cohen's *d***, so that a regression fitted in Stata and a test computed in R can be read on one axis
- A **peer panel** judges whether each pipeline is an appropriate analysis of the claim
- Only then can you ask how far apart several defensible answers are
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Standardising makes the comparison possible, but it assumes that the analysts are estimating **the same quantity**, and that the numbers fed into each conversion count the units that carry the evidence.
:::

::: {.src}
@AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness, p. 136 and Methods.
:::

::: notes
Even with the question pinned down, the answers arrive in different forms. Everyone reports the test statistic that their model happens to produce, a t, a z, an F or a chi-square, with its degrees of freedom or its sample size. Multi100 converted those onto a common scale, a partial correlation and then a standardised difference, so that a regression fitted in Stata and a test computed in R could be placed on the same axis. A peer panel judged whether each pipeline was an appropriate analysis of the claim at all, so the spread that comes out of the study is a spread among analyses that passed review. [advance] Putting several numbers on one axis takes two things for granted. One is that they estimate the same quantity, the subject of much of the second day. The other is that each conversion counts the units that actually carry the evidence, which the next slides take up.
:::

## The conversion rules {#the-conversion-rules .smaller .deepdive run="scale"}

::: {.fs-80}
| Reported statistic | Rule of the code | The number that enters beside the statistic |
|:--|:--|:--|
| *t* | $r = t / \sqrt{t^2 + df}$ | the degrees of freedom |
| *z* | $r = \tanh\left(\lvert z \rvert / \sqrt{N - 3}\right)$, with the sign of *z* | the sample size *N* |
| $\chi^2$ | $r = \tanh\left(\sqrt{\chi^2} / \sqrt{N - 3}\right)$, never negative | the sample size *N* |
| *F* | $r = \sqrt{F / (F + df_2)}$ | the second degrees of freedom |
:::

::: {.small}
Then $d = 2r / \sqrt{1 - r^2}$, and the comparison with the original result is made on *d*, within a tolerance region of ±0.05.
:::

::: {.src}
standard_correlation_coefficient.R and cohens_d.R in github.com/marton-balazs-kovacs/multi100; @AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness [p. 136].
:::

::: notes
For those who want the arithmetic, here are the rules of the code that the Multi100 team published. Each statistic is first turned into a correlation, and each rule needs a second number besides the statistic: the degrees of freedom for a t or an F, the sample size for a z or a chi-square. A chi-square carries no sign, so the correlation computed from it can never be negative, whatever the direction of the result. The correlation is then turned into a Cohen's d, and the comparison with the original study is made on d, within five hundredths either side. The article itself is candid that these transformations rely on assumptions that may not hold in every setting, and the next slide shows one of them failing.
:::

## What the conversion assumes {#what-the-conversion-assumes .smaller}

::: {.fs-80}
| Analyst of the EU-frames claim | Reported | What entered the conversion | *r* recorded |
|:--|:--|:--|--:|
| PRL47 | *z* = −3.59 | 394,575 respondents | −0.006 |
| C6HJR, my own analysis | *t* = −3.80 | 233 degrees of freedom of a model of country-years | −0.242 |
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
Almost the same statistic, and correlations about 42 times apart. Neither number is wrong for its own model. The difference comes from the conversion, which reads the units counted by the software as the units that carry the evidence.
:::

::: {.src}
The Multi100 processed data (osf.io/q5h2c), as set out on the page The Multi100 article on this site.
:::

::: notes
Here is that assumption failing, in the case that I know best, because one of the two rows is my own analysis. Two analysts of the EU-frames claim reported almost the same statistic, a z of minus three point five nine and a t of minus three point eight zero. Yet the correlations recorded for them differ by a factor of about forty-two. The z was converted on the three hundred and ninety-four thousand respondents of its analysis, and the t on the two hundred and thirty-three degrees of freedom of my model of country-years. [advance] Neither number is wrong for its own model. The difference comes from the conversion, which here treats every respondent as an independent test of a national unemployment rate, and that may well be too strong an assumption.
:::

# The four cases {#the-four-cases .darkslide background-color="rgba(0,0,0,0)"}

::: {.partlabel}
Section 3
:::

::: notes
Section three brings the programme down to the four cases of this course. Each is a claim that went through Multi100 and, to different degrees, through the rest of SCORE. We read the four claims as the analysts received them, then the conclusions reached by the five analysts of each claim, side by side, and then the record of each case across the three Rs. Your own case is one of the four. You meet it properly on the second day, so for now the aim is only to place it among the others.
:::

## The four claims {#the-four-claims .smaller}

::: {.fs-75}
| Case | The study | The claim, as Multi100 gave it |
|:----|:-------------|:-------------|
| Housing audit | A field experiment of 2017: one email to each of about a thousand public housing authorities in the United States, signed with names meant to indicate a white, a Black or a Hispanic sender | "Hispanic housing applicants were ... less likely to be greeted by name than were ... white counterparts" |
| Immigration and trust | An analysis of 2012 of rounds 1 to 4 of the European Social Survey, in 16 Western European countries | "... concerns about the effect of immigration on the national community have a(n) [reducing] impact on trust in politics" |
| Cohabitation | An analysis of 2016 of the first cohabitations of American women, begun in the early 1990s and in the late 2000s, from the National Survey of Family Growth | "cohabitors have increasingly become less likely to progress to marriage" |
| EU frames | An analysis of 2016 of sixteen Eurobarometer waves, 2004 to 2013, in 27 member states of the European Union | "... poor economic performances ... decrease positive dimensions of EU framing" |
:::

::: {.src}
@EinsteinGlick2017DoesRaceAffect, abstract; @McLaren2012CulturalDivideEurope, p. 199; @KuoRaley2016DivergingPatternsUnion, p. 932; @Teney2016DoesEUEconomic, p. 619.
:::

::: notes
Here are the four claims in the form in which Multi100 handed them to the analysts, with the dots and the square brackets of the lead team left in. The housing audit is the only experiment of the four: emails to public housing authorities in the United States that differed only in the name that signed them. The immigration-and-trust claim comes from the European Social Survey, the cohabitation claim from the union histories of American women, and the EU-frames claim from sixteen waves of the Eurobarometer. Read the claims for what they leave out as much as for what they say. The housing claim, for instance, states a direction and no size, because an ellipsis removed the twenty percentage points of the abstract.
:::

## The conclusions of the analysts {#the-conclusions-of-the-analysts}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](score-multi100_files/figure-revealjs/four-case-conclusions-1.png){width=960}
:::
:::


::: {.small}
Every analyst upheld the immigration-and-trust and the EU-frames claims, while the partial correlations recorded for those ten reanalyses run from 0.122 to 0.198 and from −0.006 to −0.452.
:::

::: {.src}
The Task 1 conclusions recorded by Multi100 [@AczelEtAl2026InvestigatingAnalyticalRobustness], as the pages SCORE and Multi100 and the opening page of this site state them.
:::

::: notes
Each row is one case and each tile one analyst, coloured by the conclusion recorded in the first task. Two of the claims were upheld by all five of their analysts, immigration and trust, and the EU frames. The housing-audit claim split three to two, with the other two analysts finding evidence neither way. The cohabitation claim split three ways: two for, one neither way and two for the opposite, all of them working on the file that Multi100 gave them. Agreeing on the conclusion is a different matter from agreeing on the number. The trust analysts recorded correlations within a narrow band, while those of the EU-frames analysts span nearly two orders of magnitude, for the reason that we met a few slides ago.
:::

## The four cases in the record {#the-four-cases-in-the-record .smaller}

::: {.fs-75}
| Case | Reproduction | Robustness (Multi100) | Replication |
|:----|:----------|:---------|:----------|
| Housing audit | never attempted, although the data and code are public | 3 of the 5 analysts for the claim, 2 neither way | a preregistered audit of 3,050 housing authorities found no significant gap |
| Immigration and trust | two reproductions from the survey files, close to the published coefficient but on fewer respondents | all 5 for the claim | two replications on later rounds of the survey, both meeting the criterion of SCORE |
| Cohabitation | none recorded | 2 for the claim, 1 neither way, 2 the opposite | tested another coefficient of the paper, which did not replicate |
| EU frames | two attempts from the original materials, neither successful | all 5 for the claim | on later Eurobarometer waves, judged not successful |
